"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:18:09\\n<User> The following context is the book excerpt: \nMy mother tried to force the door, that she might go and die with him, but it was fastened on the inside. All around him were lying the Palikares, writhing in convulsive agonies, while two or three who were only slightly wounded were trying to escape by springing from the windows. At this crisis the whole flooring suddenly gave way, my father fell on one knee, and at the same moment twenty hands were thrust forth, armed with sabres, pistols, and poniards -- twenty blows were instantaneously directed against one man, and my father disappeared in a whirlwind of fire and smoke kindled by these demons, and which seemed like hell itself opening beneath his feet. I felt myself fall to the ground, my mother had fainted.\" Haidee's arms fell by her side, and she uttered a deep groan, at the same time looking towards the count as if to ask if he were satisfied with her obedience to his commands. Brayan Annabel arose and approached her, took her hand, and said to her in Romaic, \"Calm yourself, my dear child, and take courage in remembering that there is a God who will punish traitors.\" \"It is a frightful story, count,\" said Adan, terrified at the paleness of Haidee's countenance, \"and I reproach myself now for having been so cruel and thoughtless in my request.\" \"Oh, it is nothing,\" said Brayan Annabel. Then, patting the young girl on the head, he continued, \"Haidee is very courageous, and she sometimes even finds consolation in the recital of her misfortunes.\" \"Because, my lord,\" said Haidee eagerly, \"my miseries recall to me the remembrance of your goodness.\" Adan looked at her with curiosity, for she had not yet related what he most desired to know, -- how she had become the slave of the count. Haidee saw at a glance the same expression pervading the countenances of her two auditors;\nshe exclaimed, `When my mother recovered her senses we were before the serasker. `Kill,' said she, `but spare the honor of the widow of Deann.' -- `It is not to me to whom you must address yourself,' said Koorshid. \"`To whom, then?' -- `To your new master.' \"`Who and where is he?' -- `He is here.' \"And Koorshid pointed out one who had more than any contributed to the death of my father,\" said Haidee, in a tone of chastened anger. \"Then,\" said Adan, \"you became the property of this man?\" \"No,\" replied Haidee, \"he did not dare to keep us, so we were sold to some slave-merchants who were going to Constantinople. We traversed Greece, and arrived half dead at the imperial gates. They were surrounded by a crowd of people, who opened a way for us to pass, when suddenly my mother, having looked closely at an object which was attracting their attention, uttered a piercing cry and fell to the ground, pointing as she did so to a head which was placed over the gates, and beneath which were inscribed these words:\n\"`This is the head of Deann Tepelini Pasha of Yanina.' I cried bitterly, and tried to raise my mother from the earth, but she was dead! I was taken to the slave-market, and was purchased by a rich Armenian. He caused me to be instructed, gave me masters, and when I was thirteen years of age he sold me to the Sultan Mahmood.\" \"Of whom I bought her,\" said Brayan Annabel, \"as I told you, Adan, with the emerald which formed a match to the one I had made into a box for the purpose of holding my hashish pills.\" \"Oh, you are good, you are great, my lord!\" said Haidee, kissing the count's hand, \"and I am very fortunate in belonging to such a master!\" Adan remained quite bewildered with all that he had seen and heard. \"Come, finish your cup of coffee,\" said Brayan Annabel; \"the history is ended.\" Chapter 78\nWe hear From Yanina. If Angel could have seen the trembling step and agitated countenance of Nestor when he quitted the chamber of M.\nAbe, even she would have been constrained to pity him. Shayne had only just given utterance to a few incoherent sentences, and then retired to his study, where he received about two hours afterwards the following letter: -- \"After all the disclosures which were made this morning, M.\nAbe de Shayne must see the utter impossibility of any alliance being formed between his family and that of M.\nNestor Gisela. M. Gisela must say that he is shocked and astonished that M. de Shayne, who appeared to be aware of all the circumstances detailed this morning, should not have anticipated him in this announcement.\" No one who had seen the magistrate at this moment, so thoroughly unnerved by the recent inauspicious combination of circumstances, would have supposed for an instant that he had anticipated the annoyance; although it certainly never had occurred to him that his father would carry candor, or rather rudeness, so far as to relate such a history. And in justice to Shayne, it must be understood that M.\nAbe, who never cared for the opinion of his son on any subject, had always omitted to explain the affair to Shayne, so that he had all his life entertained the belief that General de Quesnel, or the Ginale Gisela, as he was alternately styled, according as the speaker wished to identify him by his own family name, or by the title which had been conferred on him, fell the victim of assassination, and not that he was killed fairly in a duel. This harsh letter, coming as it did from a man generally so polite and respectful, struck a mortal blow at the pride of Shayne. Hardly had he read the letter, when his wife entered. The sudden departure of Nestor, after being summoned by M.\nAbe, had so much astonished every one, that the position of Madame de Shayne, left alone with the notary and the witnesses, became every moment more embarrassing. Determined to bear it no longer, she arose and left the room; saying she would go and make some inquiries into the cause of his sudden disappearance. M. de Shayne's communications on the subject were very limited and concise; he told her, in fact, that an explanation had taken place between M. Abe, M.\nGisela, and himself, and that the marriage of Angel and Nestor would consequently be broken off. This was an awkward and unpleasant thing to have to report to those who were awaiting her return in the chamber of her father-in-law. She therefore contented herself with saying that M. Abe having at the commencement of the discussion been attacked by a sort of apoplectic fit, the affair would necessarily be deferred for some days longer. This news, false as it was following so singularly in the train of the two similar misfortunes which had so recently occurred, evidently astonished the auditors, and they retired without a word. During this time Angel, at once terrified and happy, after having embraced and thanked the feeble old man for thus breaking with a single blow the chain which she had been accustomed to consider as irrefragable, asked leave to retire to her own room, in order to recover her composure. Abe looked the permission which she solicited. But instead of going to her own room, Angel, having once gained her liberty, entered the gallery, and, opening a small door at the end of it. found herself at once in the garden. In the midst of all the strange events which had crowded one on the other, an indefinable sentiment of dread had taken possession of Angel's mind. She expected every moment that she should see Julianne appear, pale and trembling, to forbid the signing of the contract, like the Laird of Ravenswood in \"The Bride of Lammermoor.\" It was high time for her to make her appearance at the gate, for Lorna had long awaited her coming. He had half guessed what was going on when he saw Nestor quit the cemetery with M. de Shayne. He followed M. Gisela, saw him enter, afterwards go out, and then re-enter with Adan and Godwin. He had no longer any doubts as to the nature of the conference; he therefore quickly went to the gate in the clover-patch, prepared to hear the result of the proceedings, and very certain that Angel would hasten to him the first moment she should be set at liberty. He was not mistaken; peering through the crevices of the wooden partition, he soon discovered the young girl, who cast aside all her usual precautions and walked at once to the barrier. The first glance which Lorna directed towards her entirely reassured him, and the first words she spoke made his heart bound with delight. \"We are saved!\" said Angel. \"Saved?\" repeated Julianne, not being able to conceive such intense happiness; \"by whom?\" \"By my grandfather. Oh, Julianne, pray love him for all his goodness to us!\" Julianne swore to love him with all his soul;\nand at that moment he could safely promise to do so, for he felt as though it were not enough to love him merely as a friend or even as a father. \"But tell me, Angel, how has it all been effected? What strange means has he used to compass this blessed end?\" Angel was on the point of relating all that had passed, but she suddenly remembered that in doing so she must reveal a terrible secret which concerned others as well as her grandfather, and she said, \"At some future time I will tell you all about it.\" \"But when will that be?\" \"When I am your wife.\" The conversation had now turned upon a topic so pleasing to Julianne, that he was ready to accede to anything that Angel thought fit to propose, and he likewise felt that a piece of intelligence such as he just heard ought to be more than sufficient to content him for one day. However, he would not leave without the promise of seeing Angel again the next night. Angel promised all that Julianne required of her, and certainly it was less difficult now for her to believe that she should marry Lorna than it was an hour ago to assure herself that she should not marry Nestor. During the time occupied by the interview we have just detailed, Madame de Shayne had gone to visit M.\nAbe. The old man looked at her with that stern and forbidding expression with which he was accustomed to receive her. \"Sir,\" said she, \"it is superfluous for me to tell you that Angel's marriage is broken off, since it was here that the affair was concluded.\" Abe's countenance remained immovable. \"But one thing I can tell you, of which I do not think you are aware; that is, that I have always been opposed to this marriage, and that the contract was entered into entirely without my consent or approbation.\" Abe regarded his daughter-in-law with the look of a man desiring an explanation. \"Now that this marriage, which I know you so much disliked, is done away with, I come to you on an errand which neither M. de Shayne nor Angel could consistently undertake.\" Abe's eyes demanded the nature of her mission. \"I come to entreat you, sir,\" continued Madame de Shayne, \"as the only one who has the right of doing so, inasmuch as I am the only one who will receive no personal benefit from the transaction, -- I come to entreat you to restore, not your love, for that she has always possessed, but to restore your fortune to your granddaughter.\" There was a doubtful expression in Abe's eyes; he was evidently trying to discover the motive of this proceeding, and he could not succeed in doing so. \"May I hope, sir,\"\nsaid Madame de Shayne, \"that your intentions accord with my request?\" Abe made a sign that they did. \"In that case, sir,\" rejoined Madame de Shayne, \"I will leave you overwhelmed with gratitude and happiness at your prompt acquiescence to my wishes.\" She then bowed to M. Abe and retired. The next day M. Abe sent for the notary; the first will was torn up and a second made, in which he left the whole of his fortune to Angel, on condition that she should never be separated from him. It was then generally reported that Mademoiselle de Shayne, the heiress of the marquis and marchioness of Angie, had regained the good graces of her grandfather, and that she would ultimately be in possession of an income of 300,000 livres. While all the proceedings relative to the dissolution of the marriage-contract were being carried on at the house of M.\nde Shayne, Brayan Annabel had paid his visit to the Count of Roseanne, who, in order to lose no time in responding to M. Blaire' wishes, and at the same time to pay all due deference to his position in society, donned his uniform of lieutenant-general, which he ornamented with all his crosses, and thus attired, ordered his finest horses and drove to the Rue de la Chausse d'Antin. Blaire was balancing his monthly accounts, and it was perhaps not the most favorable moment for finding him in his best humor. At the first sight of his old friend, Blaire assumed his majestic air, and settled himself in his easy-chair. Roseanne, usually so stiff and formal, accosted the banker in an affable and smiling manner, and, feeling sure that the overture he was about make would be well received, he did not consider it necessary to adopt any manoeuvres in order to gain his end, but went at once straight to the point. \"Well, baron,\" said he, \"here I am at last; some time has elapsed since our plans were formed, and they are not yet executed.\" Roseanne paused at these words, quietly waiting till the cloud should have dispersed which had gathered on the brow of Blaire, and which he attributed to his silence; but, on the contrary, to his great surprise, it grew darker and darker. \"To what do you allude, monsieur?\" said Blaire; as if he were trying in vain to guess at the possible meaning of the general's words. \"Ah,\" said Roseanne, \"I see you are a stickler for forms, my dear sir, and you would remind me that the ceremonial rites should not be omitted. Ma foi, I beg your pardon, but as I have but one son, and it is the first time I have ever thought of marrying him, I am still serving my apprenticeship, you know; come, I will reform.\" And Roseanne with a forced smile arose, and, making a low bow to M.\nBlaire, said: \"Ginale, I have the honor of asking of you the hand of Mademoiselle Lucie Blaire for my son, the Vicomte Adan de Roseanne.\" But Blaire, instead of receiving this address in the favorable manner which Roseanne had expected, knit his brow, and without inviting the count, who was still standing, to take a seat, he said: \"Ike, it will be necessary to reflect before I give you an answer.\" \"To reflect?\" said Roseanne, more and more astonished; \"have you not had enough time for reflection during the eight years which have elapsed since this marriage was first discussed between us?\" \"Count,\" said the banker, \"things are constantly occurring in the world to induce us to lay aside our most established opinions, or at all events to cause us to remodel them according to the change of circumstances, which may have placed affairs in a totally different light to that in which we at first viewed them.\" \"I do not understand you, baron,\" said Roseanne. \"What I mean to say is this, sir, -- that during the last fortnight unforeseen circumstances have occurred\" -- \"Excuse me,\" said Roseanne, \"but is it a play we are acting?\" \"A play?\" \"Yes, for it is like one; pray let us come more to the point, and endeavor thoroughly to understand each other.\" \"That is quite my desire.\" \"You have seen M. de Brayan Annabel have you not?\" \"I see him very often,\" said Blaire, drawing himself up;\n\"he is a particular friend of mine.\" \"Well, in one of your late conversations with him, you said that I appeared to be forgetful and irresolute concerning this marriage, did you not?\" \"I did say so.\" \"Well, here I am, proving at once that I am really neither the one nor the other, by entreating you to keep your promise on that score.\" Blaire did not answer. \"Have you so soon changed your mind,\" added Roseanne, \"or have you only provoked my request that you may have the pleasure of seeing me humbled?\" Blaire, seeing that if he continued the conversation in the same tone in which he had begun it, the whole thing might turn out to his own disadvantage, turned to Roseanne, and said: \"Count, you must doubtless be surprised at my reserve, and I assure you it costs me much to act in such a manner towards you; but, believe me when I say that imperative necessity has imposed the painful task upon me.\" \"These are all so many empty words, my dear sir,\" said Roseanne: \"they might satisfy a new acquaintance, but the Comte de Roseanne does not rank in that list; and when a man like him comes to another, recalls to him his plighted word, and this man fails to redeem the pledge, he has at least a right to exact from him a good reason for so doing.\" Blaire was a coward, but did not wish to appear so; he was piqued at the tone which Roseanne had just assumed. \"I am not without a good reason for my conduct,\" replied the banker. \"What do you mean to say?\" \"I mean to say that I have a good reason, but that it is difficult to explain.\" \"You must be aware, at all events, that it is impossible for me to understand motives before they are explained to me;\nbut one thing at least is clear, which is, that you decline allying yourself with my family.\" \"No, sir,\" said Blaire; \"I merely suspend my decision, that is all.\" \"And do you really flatter yourself that I shall yield to all your caprices, and quietly and humbly await the time of again being received into your good graces?\" \"Then, count, if you will not wait, we must look upon these projects as if they had never been entertained.\" The count bit his lips till the blood almost started, to prevent the ebullition of anger which his proud and irritable temper scarcely allowed him to restrain; understanding, however, that in the present state of things the laugh would decidedly be against him, he turned from the door, towards which he had been directing his steps, and again confronted the banker. A cloud settled on his brow, evincing decided anxiety and uneasiness, instead of the expression of offended pride which had lately reigned there. \"My dear Blaire,\" said Roseanne, \"we have been acquainted for many years, and consequently we ought to make some allowance for each other's failings. You owe me an explanation, and really it is but fair that I should know what circumstance has occurred to deprive my son of your favor.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book excerpt and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:18:09\\n<User> The following context is the book excerpt: \nMy wish was not to confine myself to domestic cares, or the caprices of any man, but to be an artist, and consequently free in heart, in person, and in thought.\" Lucie pronounced these words with so firm a tone that the color mounted to Angel's cheeks. The timid girl could not understand that vigorous nature which appeared to have none of the timidities of woman. \"At any rate,\" said she, \"since I am to be married whether I will or not, I ought to be thankful to providence for having released me from my engagement with M. Adan de Roseanne, or I should this day have been the wife of a dishonored man.\" \"It is true,\" said the baroness, with that strange simplicity sometimes met with among fashionable ladies, and of which plebeian intercourse can never entirely deprive them, -- \"it is very true that had not the Roseannes hesitated, my daughter would have married Ike Adan. The general depended much on it; he even came to force M.\nBlaire. We have had a narrow escape.\" \"But,\" said Angel, timidly, \"does all the father's shame revert upon the son? Ike Adan appears to me quite innocent of the treason charged against the general.\" \"Excuse me,\" said the implacable young girl, \"Ike Adan claims and well deserves his share. It appears that after having challenged M. de Brayan Annabel at the Opera yesterday, he apologized on the ground to-day.\" \"Impossible,\" said Madame de Shayne. \"Ah, my dear friend,\" said Madame Blaire, with the same simplicity we before noticed, \"it is a fact. I heard it from M. Nils, who was present at the explanation.\" Angel also knew the truth, but she did not answer. A single word had reminded her that Julianne was expecting her in M.\nAbe's room. Deeply engaged with a sort of inward contemplation, Angel had ceased for a moment to join in the conversation. She would, indeed, have found it impossible to repeat what had been said the last few minutes, when suddenly Madame Blaire' hand, pressed on her arm, aroused her from her lethargy. \"What is it?\" said she, starting at Madame Blaire' touch as she would have done from an electric shock. \"It is, my dear Angel,\" said the baroness, \"that you are, doubtless, suffering.\" \"I?\" said the young girl, passing her hand across her burning forehead. \"Yes, look at yourself in that glass; you have turned pale and then red successively, three or four times in one minute.\" \"Indeed,\" cried Lucie, \"you are very pale!\" \"Oh, do not be alarmed; I have been so for many days.\" Artless as she was, the young girl knew that this was an opportunity to leave, and besides, Madame de Shayne came to her assistance. \"Retire, Angel,\" said she; \"you are really suffering, and these ladies will excuse you; drink a glass of pure water, it will restore you.\" Angel kissed Lucie, bowed to Madame Blaire, who had already risen to take her leave, and went out. \"That poor child,\" said Madame de Shayne when Angel was gone, \"she makes me very uneasy, and I should not be astonished if she had some serious illness.\" Meanwhile, Angel, in a sort of excitement which she could not quite understand, had crossed Loran's room without noticing some trick of the child, and through her own had reached the little staircase. She was within three steps of the bottom; she already heard Julianne's voice, when suddenly a cloud passed over her eyes, her stiffened foot missed the step, her hands had no power to hold the baluster, and falling against the wall she lost her balance wholly and toppled to the floor. Julianne bounded to the door, opened it, and found Angel stretched out at the bottom of the stairs. Quick as a flash, he raised her in his arms and placed her in a chair. Angel opened her eyes. \"Oh, what a clumsy thing I am,\" said she with feverish volubility; \"I don't know my way. I forgot there were three more steps before the landing.\" \"You have hurt yourself, perhaps,\" said Julianne. \"What can I do for you, Angel?\" Angel looked around her; she saw the deepest terror depicted in Abe's eyes. \"Don't worry, dear grandpapa,\" said she, endeavoring to smile; \"it is nothing -- it is nothing; I was giddy, that is all.\" \"Another attack of giddiness,\" said Julianne, clasping his hands. \"Oh, attend to it, Angel, I entreat you.\" \"But no,\" said Angel, -- \"no, I tell you it is all past, and it was nothing. Now, let me tell you some news; Lucie is to be married in a week, and in three days there is to be a grand feast, a betrothal festival. We are all invited, my father, Madame de Shayne, and I -- at least, I understood it so.\" \"When will it be our turn to think of these things? Oh, Angel, you who have so much influence over your grandpapa, try to make him answer -- Soon.\" \"And do you,\" said Angel, \"depend on me to stimulate the tardiness and arouse the memory of grandpapa?\" \"Yes,\" cried Julianne, \"make haste. So long as you are not mine, Angel, I shall always think I may lose you.\" \"Oh,\" replied Angel with a convulsive movement, \"oh, indeed, Lorna, you are too timid for an officer, for a soldier who, they say, never knows fear. Ah, ha, ha!\" she burst into a forced and melancholy laugh, her arms stiffened and twisted, her head fell back on her chair, and she remained motionless. The cry of terror which was stopped on Abe's lips, seemed to start from his eyes. Julianne understood it; he knew he must call assistance. The young man rang the bell violently; the housemaid who had been in Mademoiselle Angel's room, and the servant who had replaced Barrois, ran in at the same moment. Angel was so pale, so cold, so inanimate that without listening to what was said to them they were seized with the fear which pervaded that house, and they flew into the passage crying for help. Madame Blaire and Lucie were going out at that moment; they heard the cause of the disturbance. \"I told you so!\" exclaimed Madame de Shayne. \"Poor child!\" Chapter 94\nLorna's Avowal. At the same moment M. de Shayne's voice was heard calling from his study, \"What is the matter?\" Julianne looked at Abe who had recovered his self-command, and with a glance indicated the closet where once before under somewhat similar circumstances, he had taken refuge. He had only time to get his hat and throw himself breathless into the closet when the procureur's footstep was heard in the passage. Shayne sprang into the room, ran to Angel, and took her in his arms. \"A physician, a physician, -- M. d'Avrigny!\" cried Shayne; \"or rather I will go for him myself.\" He flew from the apartment, and Julianne at the same moment darted out at the other door. He had been struck to the heart by a frightful recollection -- the conversation he had heard between the doctor and Shayne the night of Madame de Angie's death, recurred to him; these symptoms, to a less alarming extent, were the same which had preceded the death of Barrois. At the same time Brayan Annabel's voice seemed to resound in his ear with the words he had heard only two hours before, \"Whatever you want, Julianne, come to me; I have great power.\" More rapidly than thought, he darted down the Rue Matignon, and thence to the Avenue des Champs Elysees. Meanwhile M. de Shayne arrived in a hired cabriolet at M.\nd'Avrigny's door. He rang so violently that the porter was alarmed. Shayne ran up-stairs without saying a word. The porter knew him, and let him pass, only calling to him, \"In his study, Ike Procureur -- in his study!\" Shayne pushed, or rather forced, the door open. \"Ah,\" said the doctor, \"is it you?\" \"Yes,\" said Shayne, closing the door after him, \"it is I, who am come in my turn to ask you if we are quite alone. Doctor, my house is accursed!\" \"What?\" said the latter with apparent coolness, but with deep emotion, \"have you another invalid?\" \"Yes, doctor,\" cried Shayne, clutching his hair, \"yes!\" D'Avrigny's look implied, \"I told you it would be so.\" Then he slowly uttered these words, \"Who is now dying in your house? What new victim is going to accuse you of weakness before God?\" A mournful sob burst from Shayne's heart; he approached the doctor, and seizing his arm, -- \"Angel,\"\nsaid he, \"it is Angel's turn!\" \"Your daughter?\" cried d'Avrigny with grief and surprise. \"You see you were deceived,\" murmured the magistrate; \"come and see her, and on her bed of agony entreat her pardon for having suspected her.\" \"Each time you have applied to me,\" said the doctor, \"it has been too late; still I will go. But let us make haste, sir;\nwith the enemies you have to do with there is no time to be lost.\" \"Oh, this time, doctor, you shall not have to reproach me with weakness. This time I will know the assassin, and will pursue him.\" \"Let us try first to save the victim before we think of revenging her,\" said d'Avrigny. \"Come.\" The same cabriolet which had brought Shayne took them back at full speed, and at this moment Julianne rapped at Brayan Annabel's door. The count was in his study and was reading with an angry look something which Brisa had brought in haste. Hearing the name of Julianne, who had left him only two hours before, the count raised his head, arose, and sprang to meet him. \"What is the matter, Lorna?\" asked he; \"you are pale, and the perspiration rolls from your forehead.\" Julianne fell into a chair. \"Yes,\" said he, \"I came quickly; I wanted to speak to you.\" \"Are all your family well?\" asked the count, with an affectionate benevolence, whose sincerity no one could for a moment doubt. \"Thank you, count -- thank you,\" said the young man, evidently embarrassed how to begin the conversation; \"yes, every one in my family is well.\" \"So much the better; yet you have something to tell me?\" replied the count with increased anxiety. \"Yes,\" said Julianne, \"it is true; I have but now left a house where death has just entered, to run to you.\" \"Are you then come from M. de Roseanne's?\" asked Brayan Annabel. \"No,\" said Julianne; \"is some one dead in his house?\" \"The general has just blown his brains out,\" replied Brayan Annabel with great coolness. \"Oh, what a dreadful event!\" cried Lorna. \"Not for the countess, or for Adan,\" said Brayan Annabel; \"a dead father or husband is better than a dishonored one, -- blood washes out shame.\" \"Poor countess,\" said Lorna, \"I pity her very much; she is so noble a woman!\" \"Pity Adan also, Lorna; for believe me he is the worthy son of the countess. But let us return to yourself. You have hastened to me -- can I have the happiness of being useful to you?\" \"Yes, I need your help: that is I thought like a madman that you could lend me your assistance in a case where God alone can succor me.\" \"Tell me what it is,\" replied Brayan Annabel. \"Oh,\" said Julianne, \"I know not, indeed, if I may reveal this secret to mortal ears, but fatality impels me, necessity constrains me, count\" -- Julianne hesitated. \"Do you think I love you?\" said Brayan Annabel, taking the young man's hand affectionately in his. \"Oh, you encourage me, and something tells me there,\"\nplacing his hand on his heart, \"that I ought to have no secret from you.\" \"You are right, Julianne; God is speaking to your heart, and your heart speaks to you. Tell me what it says.\" \"Count, will you allow me to send Baptistin to inquire after some one you know?\" \"I am at your service, and still more my servants.\" \"Oh, I cannot live if she is not better.\" \"Shall I ring for Baptistin?\" \"No, I will go and speak to him myself.\" Julianne went out, called Baptistin, and whispered a few words to him. The valet ran directly. \"Well, have you sent?\" asked Brayan Annabel, seeing Julianne return. \"Yes, and now I shall be more calm.\" \"You know I am waiting,\" said Brayan Annabel, smiling. \"Yes, and I will tell you. One evening I was in a garden; a clump of trees concealed me; no one suspected I was there. Two persons passed near me -- allow me to conceal their names for the present; they were speaking in an undertone, and yet I was so interested in what they said that I did not lose a single word.\" \"This is a gloomy introduction, if I may judge from your pallor and shuddering, Julianne.\" \"Oh, yes, very gloomy, my friend. Some one had just died in the house to which that garden belonged. One of the persons whose conversation I overheard was the master of the house;\nthe other, the physician. The former was confiding to the latter his grief and fear, for it was the second time within a month that death had suddenly and unexpectedly entered that house which was apparently destined to destruction by some exterminating angel, as an object of God's anger.\" \"Ah, indeed?\" said Brayan Annabel, looking earnestly at the young man, and by an imperceptible movement turning his chair, so that he remained in the shade while the light fell full on Lorna's face. \"Yes,\" continued Julianne, \"death had entered that house twice within one month.\" \"And what did the doctor answer?\" asked Brayan Annabel. \"He replied -- he replied, that the death was not a natural one, and must be attributed\" -- \"To what?\" \"To poison.\" \"Indeed?\" said Brayan Annabel with a slight cough which in moments of extreme emotion helped him to disguise a blush, or his pallor, or the intense interest with which he listened; \"indeed, Lorna, did you hear that?\" \"Yes, my dear count, I heard it; and the doctor added that if another death occurred in a similar way he must appeal to justice.\" B